 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Self-heating",
   "expr": "TJ – TA = θJA × (VDD × IDD + VOL × IOUT)",
   "where": {
    "TJ": "junction temperature",
    "TA": "ambient temperature",
    "θJA": "package thermal resistance, 220.7°C/W",
    "VDD": "supply voltage",
    "IDD": "supply current",
    "VOL": "output low voltage",
    "IOUT": "output current"
   },
   "note": "IDD = 50 µA, VDD = 5.5 V, VOL = 0.3 V, IOUT = 5 mA give TJ – TA ≈ 0.4°C.",
   "page": 10
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "What is inside",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Each part combines a sensing diode on the die, a comparator, and a resistor network trimmed at the factory to one threshold. Thresholds run from -35°C to +125°C in 10°C steps. Nothing has to be configured: the threshold is fixed by the part number and only the hysteresis is chosen, by the HYST pin."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Hot and cold options",
   "page": 2,
   "text": "MCP9501/2 (option P, hot) trip as temperature rises through TSET and release once it has fallen back by the hysteresis, at THYST below TSET. MCP9503/4 (option N, cold) trip as temperature falls through TSET and release once it has risen by the hysteresis above TSET. MCP9501 and MCP9503 pull the output low when tripped (open-drain); MCP9502 and MCP9504 drive it high (push-pull). Rising-only (P) thresholds: +5°C to +125°C for MCP9501/2; +5, +15 and +25°C for MCP9503/4. Falling-only (N) thresholds for MCP9503/4: -35, -25, -15 and -5°C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Worked example of the switching points",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "With a +65°C threshold and the +2°C hysteresis: a hot-option part asserts its output (low) above +65°C and lets go below +63°C; a cold-option part asserts its output (high) below +65°C and lets go above +67°C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Hysteresis select",
   "page": 7,
   "text": "HYST is a logic input tied to GND for +2°C typical hysteresis or to VDD for +10°C typical. Its thresholds are 0.8 VDD (high) and 0.2 VDD (low)."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output types",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The push-pull output suits a microcontroller I/O or interrupt pin. The open-drain output lets several sensors at different hot spots share one wired-OR line into a single processor input, and its pull-up can go to a different rail from the sensor's supply to shift the level, for example sensors on 5 V and a pull-up to a 3 V controller supply. The switch can also drive a fan control stage with no microcontroller."
  },
  {
   "topic": "What temperature it senses",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "The part measures the voltage of a thermal diode on the die. The leads form a low thermal resistance path between the die and the board, so in practice the part follows the PCB temperature. Mounting it right next to the heat source gives the best result. Grounding pin 1 as well as pin 2 improves the heat path to the die."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Self-heating",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "With an open-drain part, the pull-up current flows in the output while it is low and warms the die; the self-heating equation gives the rise. With IDD = 50 µA, VDD = 5.5 V, VOL = 0.3 V and IOUT = 5 mA at room temperature the rise is about 0.4°C."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Decoupling",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "No external parts are required, but a 0.1 µF to 1 µF high-frequency ceramic capacitor from VDD to GND is recommended, placed as close to the power pins as possible."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Supply noise",
   "page": 11,
   "text": "The part is built not to trip falsely on high-frequency noise on its supply. With 400 mV of AC or square-wave ripple on a 5 V supply from 10 Hz to 10 MHz, TSET = +25°C and TA = +23°C, the output held steady at 5 V with no false trigger. When the supply comes from a switching regulator, or from a linear regulator fed by one (such as 3.3 V made from a switched 5 V), a 200 Ω resistor in series with VDD together with the bypass capacitor filters the switching noise."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power-up",
   "page": 3,
   "text": "Turn-on time is 1 ms typical. The thermal response to a step from +25°C air into a +125°C oil bath reaches 63% in 1.7 s typical."
  }
 ],
